1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.023
CrossSource: measuring whether a RAG system can be trusted to cite the law

2
00:00:04.623 --> 00:00:16.561
An open evaluation harness for citation accuracy in legal RAG — built to answer one question with rigor: when the system cites a court opinion, is the claim actually supported by that source?

3
00:00:17.161 --> 00:00:18.858
Corpus: 22 opinions.

4
00:00:19.138 --> 00:00:21.269
Golden set: 25 questions.

5
00:00:21.549 --> 00:00:24.173
Retrieval: BM25, top 5.

6
00:00:24.453 --> 00:00:26.957
Judge validated: 15 out of 15.

7
00:00:28.157 --> 00:00:29.183
Why this exists

8
00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:36.518
In Mata v. Avianca, lawyers were sanctioned for filing a brief full of citations an AI invented.

9
00:00:36.798 --> 00:00:45.971
Citation hallucination is the canonical trust failure of legal AI — and it is exactly the class of failure my day job revolves around.

10
00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:55.560
CrossSource is the public, reproducible version of that work: a fixed corpus, a golden question set, and an evaluation pipeline anyone can run.

11
00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.381
Method

12
00:00:57.981 --> 00:01:11.441
Corpus: 22 public-domain U S court opinions, 16 Supreme Court, 2 federal circuit and 4 state, sourced from Harvard Law School's Caselaw Access Project and chunked to about 350 words.

13
00:01:11.721 --> 00:01:14.345
Retrieval: BM25, top 5.

14
00:01:14.625 --> 00:01:21.292
Generation: Claude, with mandatory inline citations in doc ID, colon, chunk ID format.

15
00:01:21.572 --> 00:01:27.350
Golden set: 25 human-authored questions with verified ground-truth supporting chunks.

16
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:36.613
Two configurations: baseline, which is citation formatting only, versus strict, which adds explicit citation-discipline rules to the prompt.

17
00:01:36.893 --> 00:01:46.329
Scoring: claim-level citation precision and recall, faithfulness, and answer relevance, scored by an LLM judge and validated by hand.

18
00:01:47.529 --> 00:01:48.277
Findings

19
00:01:48.877 --> 00:01:51.011
Table 1, baseline versus strict.

20
00:01:51.291 --> 00:01:57.605
Citation precision: 0.981 in baseline, 0.994 in strict.

21
00:01:57.885 --> 00:02:01.449
Citation recall: 0.760 in both.

22
00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:04.955
Faithfulness: 1.000 in both.

23
00:02:05.235 --> 00:02:11.472
Answer relevance: 0.980 in baseline, 0.960 in strict.

24
00:02:12.072 --> 00:02:15.212
Table 2, failure taxonomy by configuration.

25
00:02:15.492 --> 00:02:19.276
Right document, wrong passage: 3 in baseline, 1 in strict.

26
00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:21.566
Missing authority: 6 in both.

27
00:02:21.846 --> 00:02:23.891
Wrong document: 0 in both.

28
00:02:24.171 --> 00:02:26.335
Unsupported claim: 0 in both.

29
00:02:26.935 --> 00:02:38.573
The finding that matters: prompting discipline buys precision — wrong-passage citations drop 3 → 1, precision rises 0.981 → 0.994.

30
00:02:38.853 --> 00:02:48.967
It cannot buy recall: 0.760 in both configurations, because every missing-authority failure traces to retrieval, not generation.

31
00:02:49.247 --> 00:02:53.869
Knowing which layer owns the failure is the entire point of separating the metrics.

32
00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:57.669
The judge caught a bug — because I checked the judge

33
00:02:58.269 --> 00:03:03.573
I hand-graded a blind, stratified sample of the judge's citation-precision verdicts:

34
00:03:04.173 --> 00:03:07.080
100% agreement (15/15).

35
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:19.396
That validation pass also surfaced a genuine harness defect — consecutive citations produced punctuation-only claim spans that the judge initially mislabeled as failures.

36
00:03:19.676 --> 00:03:21.327
Fixing it changed the metrics.

37
00:03:21.927 --> 00:03:31.614
That is the lesson worth paying for: an unvalidated eval reports wrong numbers with full confidence — precisely the failure mode the eval exists to catch.

38
00:03:32.814 --> 00:03:34.031
What this demonstrates

39
00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:36.752
The same discipline I run in production:

40
00:03:37.352 --> 00:03:38.668
Instrument the pipeline.

41
00:03:38.948 --> 00:03:41.852
Build a failure taxonomy instead of a single score.

42
00:03:42.132 --> 00:03:43.799
Validate the judge externally.

43
00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:48.467
Separate precision from recall, so you know whether to fix prompting or retrieval.

